{"kind":"symbol","primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"GraphAdaptor","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A7AdaptorP4BaseQa","text":"Base","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/GraphAdaptor\/Base-swift.associatedtype"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Views (a transposed view, a subgraph view, a directed or undirected view) and wrappers that add"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rules (a multigraph, an acyclic graph) are adaptors. Every adaptor gets two things:"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Its ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"generation"},{"text":" is the base’s, so a structure derived from the adaptor is current exactly","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"when one derived from the base would be.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"When the base is a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PropertyGraph"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"values(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[map, of: vertex]"},{"type":"text","text":" read the base’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex maps. When the adaptor’s edge positions are the base’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Edges.Index == Base.EdgePosition"},{"type":"text","text":"), "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[map, ofEdgeAt: position]"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"values(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" read the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"base’s edge maps too. A view whose positions are not the base’s, such as a complement, has no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge-map reads. These members are read-only. A "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GraphDelegator"},{"type":"text","text":" also writes."}]}]}],"type":"unorderedList"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A view whose vertices are a subset of its base’s should declare its own ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[map, of: vertex]"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that traps on a vertex outside the view. The default reads the base’s value.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Nothing else is forwarded. A view’s graph members are its own, or the protocol defaults over"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"its own members. "},{"code":"GraphDelegator","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" forwards every member to the base, and only a wrapper that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"keeps the base’s adjacency conforms to it. For a view such as a transposed one, the base’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"members would describe the wrong graph."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A type that wraps a base graph and passes its positions through."}],"metadata":{"title":"GraphAdaptor","role":"symbol","modules":[{"name":"GraphProtocols"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"GraphAdaptor"}],"externalID":"s:14GraphProtocols0A7AdaptorP","symbolKind":"protocol","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"GraphAdaptor","kind":"identifier"}],"roleHeading":"Protocol"},"sections":[],"topicSections":[{"title":"Associated Types","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/GraphAdaptor\/Base-swift.associatedtype"],"anchor":"Associated-Types"},{"title":"Instance Properties","generated":true,"identifie